    <h2>The gap most test prep never trains</h2>

    <h3>Sound familiar?</h3>
    <p>Maybe your teen does well on practice tests, then falls apart on the real one. Or they understand the math at the kitchen table and blank on it once the timer starts.</p>
    <p>Or math itself has become the problem: they shut down before they've even started the homework.</p>

    <h3>Why it happens</h3>
    <p>That's not a knowledge gap. It's the one thing most test prep doesn't train: what happens to their thinking once the pressure's real.</p>

    <h3>Why I built this</h3>
    <p>I'm Joe Ruotolo. I've worked with a lot of students, and I've seen months of preparation come undone because a student walked into the test dysregulated, with no tools to get their head back in the game.</p>
    <p>I built Third Mind Learning to solve that problem, so students walk in with both the knowledge and the state-management skills they need to perform at their best.</p>

    <figure class="quote">
      <blockquote>
        <p>&ldquo;The heart coherence work made a real difference. It improved my focus and helped me manage the stress and anxiety I used to feel about testing.&rdquo;</p>
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption>&mdash; Mason G, SAT Student</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</div>

